# eval-harness — core rules

SPEC.md is the source of truth. Read the section a task names before writing code.
If a request conflicts with SPEC.md or these rules, say so and stop. Do not work around it.

## What this repo is
A model-swap eval pipeline for an audit revenue-testing agent. The agent is small on
purpose (5 nodes). The eval layer is the product: scorers, runner, gate, feedback loop,
scorecard. If a change makes the agent smarter but the evals no weaker, it is out of scope.

## Must run offline
- `make demo` runs with no API keys, no Docker, no network, in under 60 seconds.
- Real model providers and LangSmith are optional, switched on only by env vars
  (see SPEC.md §8). Code paths that need a key read it at the point of use and raise a
  clear error if missing. Never at import time.
- Never call a network API from tests. Tests use stub models or the replay cache.

## Data honesty
- Synthetic data only. Invented company, invented people, invented documents.
  No real company, client, auditor, or product names in data, prompts, or fixtures.
- Never hardcode a metric, score, price, or result in README, templates, or code.
  Every number shown comes from a run artifact under `runs/`.
- Stub-model results are simulated. Anything rendered from a stub run carries the
  `simulated: true` flag and a visible SIMULATED label. Never strip it.
- Model prices live only in `config/models.toml`, each with `source_url` and `as_of`.
  A real model with no price entry is an error, not $0.

## Names
- Python package is `auditlab`. Do not create a module named after a banned name.
- Banned names are listed only in the local, gitignored `.names-denylist`. They must not
  appear anywhere in tracked files (README and docs included). `make names` runs
  `scripts/check_names.sh` against that list (skipped when the list is absent, as in CI);
  it has no allowlist. Never add one, and never copy the list into a tracked file.
- Docs never say where any code came from.
- No claims of affiliation with any real company.

## Money and numbers
- Money is integer cents (`int`). Model cost is integer USD micros. Never `float` for
  money. Convert at the parse boundary only.
- Arithmetic (tie-outs, totals, deltas) is deterministic Python, never an LLM.

## Style
- Python 3.12, full type hints, Pydantic v2 models, `ruff check` and `mypy --strict` clean.
- Pure functions for scorers and gate logic: no I/O, no network, no randomness inside.
- Side effects (files, network, tracing export) only in `auditlab/models/`, `auditlab/telemetry.py`,
  `evals/runner.py`, `feedback/`, `report/`, and CLI entry points.
- Modules under ~200 lines. Split rather than grow.
- Fail loudly. No silent fallbacks, no "N/A", no placeholder values. Raise with a message
  that says what is missing and how to fix it.
- Seeded randomness only (`random.Random(seed)`); never the global RNG.
- No notebooks. No JavaScript frameworks. No CDN links (the scorecard must open offline).

## Working style
- Plan before diffing. For anything touching more than one file, list the files and the
  change, then wait for approval.
- Failing test first, then implementation.
- One task per change. Do not refactor across modules opportunistically.
- Do not add dependencies beyond SPEC.md §3 without asking.
- Never edit committed answer keys under `data/engagements/` by hand; regenerate them
  from `data/generate.py`.
- Never edit `evals/baselines/production.json` to make the gate pass. Re-baselining is an
  explicit command (`make baseline`) the human runs.
